If  $BX$  intersects  $(XKOL)$  again at  $Y$ , then  $\angle YLK=\angle BXK=\angle BCA$ , so  $YL\parallel BC$ . Similarly,  $YK\parallel BA$ , so  $ABC$  and  $KYL$  are homothetic. This homothety from  $KYL$  to  $ABC$  maps  $X$  to  $X'$  on  $(ABC)$  on line  $BX$ . If the center of homothety is  $Z$ , then  $\frac{ZK}{ZL}=\frac{ZA}{ZC}=\frac{AK}{CL}$ . Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{KX}{XL}&=\frac{AX'}{X'C}
&=\frac{\sin\angle ABZ}{\sin\angle CBZ}
&=\frac{AZ}{CZ}\frac{BC}{BA}
&=\frac{AK}{CL}\frac{BC}{BA}
&=\frac{AB}{BD}KD\frac{BD}{BC}\frac1{DL}\frac{BC}{BA}
