We claim that the maximum number of “FOR” votes the chairperson can obtain is  $68$ . It is possible as shown below (black squares represent the  $28$  supporters, gray squares represent other leaders who voted “FOR”, white squares represent leaders who voted "AGAINST"):
[asy]
size(150);
int n = 9;
// Fill cells first
for (int x = 0; x < n; ++x) {
  for (int y = 0; y < n; ++y) {
    int s = x + y;
    if (s % 2 == 1) {
      fill((x,y)--(x+1,y)--(x+1,y+1)--(x,y+1)--cycle, gray); // light gray
    } else if (x % 2 == 0 && y % 2 == 0 && s % 4 == 0) {
      fill((x,y)--(x+1,y)--(x+1,y+1)--(x,y+1)--cycle, white); // white
    } else {
      fill((x,y)--(x+1,y)--(x+1,y+1)--(x,y+1)--cycle, black); // black
    }
  }
}
// Draw gridlines on top
for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
  draw((i,0)--(i,n), black+0.7bp); // vertical lines
  draw((0,i)--(n,i), black+0.7bp); // horizontal lines
}
[/asy]
Now assume there exists a configuration that has at least  $69$  "FOR" votes. We call an edge *special* if it is between a black and gray square. Let  $a$  be the number of black squares on the boundary of the  $9 \times 9$  grid. There are at most  $3$  special edges for each boundary black square and at most  $4$  special edges for other black squares. Thus, the number of special edges is at most  $3a + 4(28-a) = 112-a$ . Now let  $b$  be the number of gray squares on the boundary of the  $9 \times 9$  grid. There are at least  $2$  special edges for each boundary gray square and at least  $3$  special edges for other gray squares. Note that the total number of gray squares is at least  $69 - 28 = 41$ . Thus, the number of special edges is at least  $2b + 3(41 - b) = 123 - b$ . Therefore, we have  $123 - b \le 112 - a \Leftrightarrow b - a \ge 11$ . 

Each gray square on the boundary of the  $9 \times 9$  grid must be adjacent to at least one black square on the boundary, and each black square on the boundary is adjacent to at most  $2$  gray squares on the boundary. Thus, we must have  $b \le 2a$ . Then  $11 \le b - a \le b/2$ , implying  $b \ge 22$  and  $a \ge b/2 \ge 11$ . So  $a + b \ge 33$ , however, there are only  $32$  boundary squares, contradiction.

Therefore, the largest possible number of "FOR" votes is  $\boxed{68}$ .

