Let  $(a_0,b_0):=(x+y+z,\tfrac{1}{x}+\tfrac{1}{y}+\tfrac{1}{z})$  where  $xyz=1$ ; inductively 
\begin{align*}
a_n&=x^{2^n}+y^{2^n}+z^{2^n}
b_n&=x^{-2^n}+y^{-2^n}+z^{-2^n}.
\end{align*}
Observe  $\max(|x|,|y|,|z|)>1$ , as  $a_0>b_0$  prevents equality; and so if  $x,y,z\in\mathbb{R}$  we are clearly done. Otherwise, as  $a_0,b_0$  are real, we may substitute  $(x,y,z):=(re^{\theta i},re^{-\theta i},\tfrac{1}{r^2})$  where  $r>0$  to find
\[a_n=2r^{2^n}\cos(2^n\theta)+r^{-2^{n+1}}.\]
If  $r<1$  we are again done; if  $r>1$ , here  $a_0>b_0$  translates as  $\cos\theta>\tfrac{1}{2}(r+\tfrac{1}{r})$ , bad.  $\square$

