The answer is  $2$ , which is achieved by the tuple  $s(n) \coloneq (2^0, 2^1, 2^2, \dots, 2^{n-2}, 2^{n-2})$ . Let  $f(n)$  be the maximum attained value for  $n$  variables. Furthermore, write  $d_i \coloneq \operatorname{gcd}(a_i, a_{i+1})$ , where  $d_0 = 1$  to be the sequence of the greatest common divisors. We finish the problem with the following claim. **<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $f(n)$  is at most  $f(2) = 2$  for all  $n$ . 

*Proof.* It is clear that  $\operatorname{lcm}(d_{i-1}, d_i) \mid a_i$ , so we quickly have the bound  $\operatorname{lcm}(d_{i-1}, d_i) \le a_i$ . Note that we may shift  $a_n$  down to  $d_{n-1}$  without changing the sequence  $(d_i)$ , and also resulting in a greater sum. Therefore, \begin{align*} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{a_i} &\le \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \left( \frac{1}{\operatorname{lcm}(d_{i-1}, d_i)} \right) + \frac{1}{d_{n-1}}   &=  \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \frac{\operatorname{gcd}(d_{i-1}, d_i)}{d_{i-1}d_i} + \frac{1}{d_{n-1}}  &\le \sum_{i=2}^n \frac{d_i - d_{i-1}}{d_{i-1}d_i} + \frac{1}{d_{n-1}} + 1  &= \frac{1}{d_1} + 1 \le 2\end{align*}
where the last equality comes from telescoping. Therefore, we can "collapse" the terms down to what's essentially the  $n = 2$  case, giving the desired bound.  $\square$

