Define the function  $g_n (x) = 2n \{x\}.$  We start with the following:**Claim:** For all  $m \ge 2$  we have   $$ n^k = \sum_{i=1}^{m-1} (2n)^{k-i} \left\lfloor g_n^{i-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\rfloor + (2n)^{k-m+1} \left\{ g_n^{m-2} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\} $$  *Proof:* By induction on  $m.$  The base case  $m=2$  is because   $$ (2n)^{k-1} \left(\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right\rfloor + \left\{ \frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right\}\right) = (2n)^{k-1} \cdot \frac{n}{2^{k-1}} = n^k. $$   Now for the inductive step, we have
\begin{align*}
 (2n)^{k-m+1} \left\{ g_n^{m-2} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\} &= (2n)^{k-m} \cdot 2n \left\{ g_n^{m-2} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\} 
  &= (2n)^{k-m} g_n^{m-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) 
  &= (2n)^{k-m} \left\lfloor g_n^{m-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\rfloor + (2n)^{k-m} \left\{ g_n^{m-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\}.
\end{align*}
Plugging this into the inductive hypothesis completes the induction.

Using  $m = k+1$  in the above claim gives   $$ n^k = \sum_{i=1}^{k} (2n)^{k-i} \left\lfloor g_n^{i-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\rfloor + \left\{ g_n^{k-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\}. $$   Every term to the left of the rightmost term is an integer, so the rightmost term is also an integer. However, since  $0 \le \{x\} < 1$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{R},$  it must be  $0.$  This gives us   $$ \boxed{n^k = \sum_{i=1}^{k} (2n)^{k-i} \left\lfloor g_n^{i-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\rfloor}. $$   We have   $$ \left\lfloor g_n^{i-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\rfloor < 2n $$   since  $g(x) < 2n$  for all  $x.$  Thus the boxed equation gives the unique base  $2n$  representation of  $n^k.$ **Claim:** We have that  $2^{k-i-1} g_n^{i} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right)$  is an odd integer, for all  $0 \le i \le k-2.$ *Proof:* Again by induction, this time on  $i.$  The base case  $i=0$  is trivial as  $n$  is odd. For the inductive step, we have  $g_n^i \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) = \frac{z}{2^{k-i-1}}$  for some odd integer  $z,$  so   $$ 2^{k-i-2} g_n^{i+1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) = 2^{k-i-1} n \left\{\frac{z}{2^{k-i-1}}\right\} = 2^{k-i-1} n \left(\frac{z}{2^{k-i-1}} - \left\lfloor \frac{z}{2^{k-i-1}} \right\rfloor \right). $$   This is   $$ nz - 2^{k-i-1} n \left\lfloor \frac{z}{2^{k-i-1}} \right\rfloor. $$   The last term is even for  $i < k-1,$  and the first term is odd since  $n$  and  $z$  are odd. This completes the induction.

Now, when  $i = 1,$  we have   $$ \left\lfloor g_n^{i-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\rfloor = \left\lfloor\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right\rfloor, $$   so assume  $i \ge 2.$  Then we have
\begin{align*}
 \left\lfloor g_n^{i-1} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\rfloor &= \left\lfloor 2n \left\{g_n^{i-2} \left(\frac{n}{2^{k-1}}\right) \right\}\right\rfloor 
 &\ge \left\lfloor 2n \cdot \frac{1}{2^{k-i+1}}\right\rfloor 
 &= \left\lfloor \frac{n}{2^{k-i}} \right\rfloor
\end{align*}
 since any odd positive integer is at least  $1.$  Therefore, the  $i$ th digit from the left in the base  $2n$  representation of  $n^k$  is at least  $\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2^{k-i}}\right\rfloor.$  Taking  $n > (d+1) \cdot 2^{k-1}$  finishes.

We will assume  $k$  is sufficiently large for the sake of convenience. The proof works for all values of  $k$ . Note that  $n^k \equiv 1 \mod{2}, n^k \equiv 0 \mod{n} \implies n^k \equiv n \mod{2n}$ . Now, we must have  $n^k \mod{4n^2} - n > 2nd$ , we have that  $n^k \equiv 0 \mod{n^2}, n^k \equiv o \mod{4}$  for some  $o \in \{1,3\}$ . Thus we have  $n^2t \equiv o \mod{4} \implies t \equiv o' \mod{4}$  for some odd  $o'$ . So  $n^k = n^2(4l+o') \equiv o'n^2 \mod{4n^2}$  where  $3n^2 \geq o'n^2 \geq n^2$ . Thus we must have  $n^2-n > 2nd \implies n>2d+1$ . 


Note that following this method we always have that 
\begin{align*}
n^k \mod{(2n)^t}-n^k \mod{(2n)^{t-1}} &\geq n^t - (2^{t-1}-1)n^{t-1} 
&> (2n)^{t-1}d 
\implies n-2^{t-1}+1&>2^{t-1}d 
\implies n&>2^{t-1}(d+1)-1
\end{align*}
We want this inequality to be satisfied for all  $t$  from  $1$  to the number of digits of  $n_{2n}$ . So we will just take  $N=2^{k-1}(d+1)-1$  since certainly  $n^k$  would have less than  $k$  digits in base  $2n$ , then every odd  $n>N$  would satisfy the condition.

We can check that the units digit must take on the value  $n$  by taking  $\mod{2n}$  on  $n^k$  (so  $a_0 = n > d$  for sufficiently large  $n$ ). Consider the  $i$ th digit of the representation (the one corresponding to  $(2n)^i$ ). Assume all of  $0,1, \cdots i-1$  digit places below it satisfy the bound  $a_j > d$ . Those  $i - 1$  digits have the upper bound

\[
(2n-1) \frac{(2n)^{i} - 1}{2n - 1} = (2n)^i - 1
\]

Next, taking modulo  $(2n)^{i+1}$  on  $n^{k}$ , we have by CRT: 

\begin{align*}
    n^{k} \equiv 0 \pmod{n^{i+1}} 
    n^{k} \equiv x \pmod{2^{i+1}}
\end{align*}

Hence we have  $x$  take the values  $n^{i+1}, 3n^{i+1} \cdots (2^{i+1}-1)n^{k}$ . The sum of the first  $i$  digits would be lower bounded by  $n^{i+1}$ . So, the value of the  $i$ th digit is lower bounded: (we've lower bounded the sum of all the digits from  $0$  to  $i$  and upper bounded the sum of all the digits from  $0$  to  $i-1$  as to lower bound the  $i$ th term)

\[
n^{i+1} - \left((2n)^{i} - 1\right) \leq a_i(2n)^{i}
\]

and this finishes because  $n^{i+1}$  has a higher degree than the rest of the terms in the inequality. In particular, for sufficiently large  $n$ , it always holds that  $a_i$  takes a value greater than  $d$ .

