Grant apparently used inversion, projective, and Brokard to solve this. I present to you all an even more overkill solution. 

Let  $Z$  be the foot from  $A$  to  $BC$ . PoP four times gives the identities \begin{align*}
BX \cdot BY = BZ \cdot BC,
ZX \cdot ZY = BZ \cdot CZ.
\end{align*}

Obviously, finding the power at  $C$  with respect to  $(AFP)$  would be nice, so how do we do this? The answer should be obvious: **Synthetic!!!** Just kidding. **Linearity of Power of a Point!!!**

It should be remarked that a coordinate bash here also suffices because the two above equations uniquely determine  $X$  and  $Y$  up to swapping. In fact, this should be called a number-line bash because we don't even care about the  $y$ -coordinates. 

Anyways, define  $f(Q) = QZ^2 - \text{Pow}(Q, (AFP))$  for any point  $Q$ . This is a linear function because  $QZ^2$  is the power of  $Q$  with respect to the circle of radius  $0$  centered at  $Z$ . We can easily compute  $f$  at  $B$  and  $Z$ , and use the fact that  $f$  is a linear function to find that the power of  $C$  with respect to  $(AFP)$  is  $-CB \cdot CZ$ . Thus, \begin{align*}
CX \cdot CY = CB \cdot CZ = CH \cdot CF.
\end{align*}

We're actually basically done here. Reflect  $H$  about  $C$  to get  $P'$  and observe that  $P'$  lies on  $(AFP)$  by the converse of PoP. Because  $PP'$  and  $XY$  are parallel and  $P, P', X, Y$  are concyclic, we conclude that the perpendicular bisectors of  $PP'$  and  $XY$  are the same line. Finally,  $C$  clearly lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $PP'$  (because it is the circumcenter of  $HPP'$ ), and hence  $CX = CY$ .

