\begin{align*}
\angle CPX &= \angle CPD + \angle DPX 
                     &= \angle ABC + \angle XFB 
                     &\equiv \angle FBX + \angle XFB = \angle FXC.
\end{align*}
It follows that  $\triangle FXC\sim\triangle XPC$ .  In turn,
\[
CX^2 = FC\cdot CP = FC\cdot HC = BC\cdot DC.
\]
Subtract  $DC^2$  from both sides to obtain
\[
CX^2 - CD^2 = BD\cdot DC.\qquad(\dagger)
\]
Finally, note from radical axis that  $BD\cdot DC = DX\cdot DY$ , so  $(\dagger)$  rewrites to
\[
CX^2 - CD^2 = DX\cdot DY.
\]
But recall by difference of squares that  $MX^2 - MD^2 = DX\cdot DY$ , where  $M$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{XY}$ .  It follows that  $M\equiv C$ .

