Its well-known that  $P$  lies on  $\odot(ABC)$ . The crux of the problem is to show that  $Q$  lies on  $\odot(AFP)$ . This is a consequence of of the Intersecting Chords Theorem, observe that 
    \[AD \cdot HD = FH \cdot HC \implies AD \cdot 2HD = FH \cdot 2CH \implies AH \cdot HP = FH \cdot HQ\]
which proves the claim. Since  $P$  is the reflection of  $H$  over  $D$ , we get 
    \[\overline{CP} = \overline{CH} = \overline{CQ}\]
which means  $C$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord  $PQ$  in  $\odot(AFP)$ . This is enough to conclude, since chord  $XY$  is parallel to  $PQ$ , both the chords will share the same perpendicular bisector. This finishes the solution.  $\blacksquare$

