Let  $P=(p:q:r)$ . Then,  $K=(x:q:r)$ , and  $K$  lies on the circumcircle of  $ABC$ , which means that $-a^2qr-b^2xr-c^2xq=0$ , so  $x=\frac{-a^2qr}{b^2r+c^2q}$ . Therefore,  $$ K=(-a^2qr:q(b^2r+c^2q):r(b^2r+c^2q)). $$  Now, we compute the point  $Q$ . We have  $X=(p:q:0)$  and  $Y=(p:0:r)$ , so the circumcircle of  $AXY$  has equation  $$ -a^2yz-b^2zx-c^2xy+(x+y+z)\left(\frac{c^2p}{p+q}y+\frac{b^2p}{p+r}z\right). $$   This intersects the circumcircle of  $ABC$ , which has equation  $-a^2yz-b^2zx-c^2xy=0$ , on the line  $\frac{c^2p}{p+q}y+\frac{b^2p}{p+r}z=0$ , so  $Q=(x:-b^2(p+q):c^2(p+r))$ . Plugging this point into the equation for the circumcircle of  $ABC$  gives  $$ a^2b^2c^2(p+q)(p+r)-b^2c^2x(p+r)+b^2c^2x(p+q)=0, $$   so  $x=\frac{a^2(p+q)(p+r)}{r-q}$ . Therefore,  $$ Q=(a^2(p+q)(p+r):-b^2(p+q)(r-q):c^2(p+r)(r-q)). $$  Let  $ux+vy+wz=\begin{vmatrix}Q_x&Q_y&Q_zK_x&K_y&K_zx&y&z\end{vmatrix}$  be an expression which is zero for all points on line  $QK$ . The equation of the circumcircle of  $QPK$  has the form  $-a^2yz-b^2zx-c^2xy+k(x+y+z)(ux+vy+wz)=0$  for some real  $k$ . Then, substituting the coordinates of  $P$  into this equation implies  $$ a^2qr+b^2rp+c^2pq=k(p+q+r)\begin{vmatrix}Q_x&Q_y&Q_zK_x&K_y&K_zp&q&r\end{vmatrix}, $$   and we want to show that the power of the midpoint of  $BC$  is equal to  $\frac14a^2$ , which is equivalent to  $$ -a^2+2k\begin{vmatrix}Q_x&Q_y&Q_zK_x&K_y&K_z0&1&1\end{vmatrix}=a^2. $$  We have
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix}a^2(p+q)(p+r)&-b^2(p+q)(r-q)&c^2(p+r)(r-q)-a^2qr&q(b^2r+c^2q)&r(b^2r+c^2q)p&q&r\end{vmatrix}&=\begin{vmatrix}a^2(p+q)(p+r)&-b^2(p+q)(r-q)&c^2(p+r)(r-q)-a^2qr-p(b^2r+c^2q)&0&0p&q&r\end{vmatrix}
&=(a^2qr+b^2rp+c^2pq)\begin{vmatrix}-b^2(p+q)(r-q)&c^2(p+r)(r-q)q&r\end{vmatrix}
&=(a^2qr+b^2rp+c^2pq)(q-r)(b^2(p+q)r-c^2(p+r)q).
\end{align*}

Therefore,   $$ k=\frac1{(q-r)(p+q+r)(b^2(p+q)r-c^2(p+r)q)}, $$   so we want to show  $\begin{vmatrix}Q_x&Q_y&Q_zK_x&K_y&K_z0&1&1\end{vmatrix}=a^2(q-r)(p+q+r)(b^2(p+q)r-c^2(p+r)q)$ . This is true because
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix}a^2(p+q)(p+r)&-b^2(p+q)(r-q)&c^2(p+r)(r-q)-a^2qr&q(b^2r+c^2q)&r(b^2r+c^2q)0&1&1\end{vmatrix}&=a^2qr(q-r)(b^2(p+q)+c^2(p+r))+a^2(p+q)(p+r)(b^2r+c^2q)(q-r)
&=a^2(q-r)(b^2qr(p+q)+c^2qr(p+r)+b^2r(p+q)(p+r)+c^2q(p+q)(p+r))
&=a^2(q-r)(b^2r(p+q)(p+q+r)+c^2q(p+r)(p+q+r))
&=a^2(q-r)(p+q+r)(b^2r(p+q)+c^2q(p+r)).
\end{align*}

Therefore, the midpoint of  $BC$  always has power  $\frac14a^2$  with respect to the circumcircle of  $QPK$ , so it is the common radical center of all of the circles.

