Note that the Newton Gauss Line of quadrilateral  $ABPCXY$  passes through  $L$ ,  $N$ , and  $M$ . Denote  $S$  as the second intersection of  $AP$  and  $(AXY)$ . From spiral similarity, we can get  $\triangle QXY \sim \triangle QSK \sim \triangle QNM \sim \triangle QYC$ . Here, the center of spiral similarity is  $Q$ . Therefore,   $$ \angle(KS, MN) = \angle (KQ, MQ) $$   This implies that  $Q, L, M, K$  are concyclic. Now, this means that  $(ABC)$ ,  $(QPK)$ , and  $(QLMK)$  are coaxal. Hence, from coaxiality lemma, the following holds. (Note that  $L$  is the midpoint of  $AP$ , so  $LA = - LP$ .   $$ -1 = \dfrac{\mathbf{Pow}_{(ABC)}(L)}{\mathbf{Pow}_{(QPK)}(L)} =  \dfrac{\mathbf{Pow}_{(ABC)}(M)}{\mathbf{Pow}_{(QPK)}(M)} $$  This means that  $\mathbf{Pow}_{(QPK)}(M) = - MB \cdot MC$ , which is constant. Therefore, for any choice of  $P$ ,  $M$  is a radical center with fixed power  $-MB \cdot MC$ .

