Here is a not so elegant solution:
<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>Consider the following equation,  $z^2 - y^2 = x$ . Naturally, 
\begin{align*}
z^2 \equiv 0 \pmod{4} \lor z^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{4} 
y^2 \equiv 0 \pmod{4} \lor y^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{4} 
\end{align*}

Clearly, only the following differences are possible:
\begin{align*}
z^2 - y^2 &\equiv 0 &\pmod{4} 
z^2 - y^2 &\equiv 1 &\pmod{4} 
z^2 - y^2 &\equiv -1 \equiv 3 &\pmod{4}
\end{align*}
Thus,  $a \equiv 2 \pmod{4}$  will never equal to the difference of squares. Naturally, the residue set's modulus is  $\infty$ .</details>

