Engineers solution:

Notice that the sum is just \[\left(4^6 \cdot \prod_{n = 1}^6 \sin \left(\frac{90n}{7}\right)\right)^2\]

we can see that 
\begin{align} 
\prod_{n = 1}^1 \sin \left(\frac{90n}{2}\right) &= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} 
\prod_{n = 1}^2 \sin \left(\frac{90n}{3}\right) &= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} 
\prod_{n = 1}^3 \sin \left(\frac{90n}{7}\right) &= \frac{1}{4} = \frac{\sqrt{4}}{8} 
\end{align}

Thus \[\prod_{n = 1}^6 \sin \left(\frac{90n}{7}\right) = \frac{\sqrt{7}}{64}\] This means that we just have   $$ (64\sqrt{7})^2 = 4096 \cdot 7 \equiv \boxed{672} \pmod{1000} $$

