So using the law of sines on  $ \bigtriangleup ADE$ , 

\begin{align*}
AD  &= AE \cdot \dfrac{\sin(2\alpha)}{\sin(180^\circ-3\alpha)}
    &= AE \cdot \dfrac{\sin(2\alpha)}{\sin(\alpha+2\alpha)}
    &= 10 \cdot \dfrac{\sin(2\alpha)}{\sin(\alpha)\cos(2\alpha)+\cos(\alpha)\sin(2\alpha)}
    &= 10 \cdot \dfrac{\sin(2\alpha)}{\sqrt{\dfrac{1-\cos(2\alpha)}{2}}\cdot\cos(2\alpha)+\sqrt{\dfrac{1+\cos(2\alpha)}{2}}\cdot\sin(2\alpha)}
    &= 10 \cdot \dfrac{\dfrac{12}{13}}{\sqrt{\dfrac{8}{26}}\cdot\dfrac{5}{13}+\sqrt{\dfrac{18}{26}}\cdot\dfrac{12}{13}}
    &= 10 \cdot \dfrac{12\sqrt{13}}{10+36} = \dfrac{60\sqrt{13}}{23}
\end{align*}
 $ \therefore$  the answer is  $ 60+13+23=\boxed{086}$ .

@above your solution may be a little bit overkill but it sure gets the job done.

We first make this observation: we have that  $x, y, z \ge 1$ , so let's subtract  $4 + 3 + 2$  from  $2009$  to make it a bit easier. Then we get that  $4x + 3y + 2z = 2000$  for  $x, y, z \ge 0$ . That looks awfully similar to the second equation. And it is almost the exact same, except the only difference is that our modified equation allows for solutions where  $x,y,z=0$ . So, we just need to find the number of solutions where at least one of  $x,y,z$  is equal to  $0$ .**Case 1**:    $x=0$ This gives  $3y + 2z = 2000$ . Note we don't want  $y=0, z=0$  otherwise we are overcounting some later. Note that  $2 \mid y$ , therefore we get even solutions from  $y=2$  to  $y=666$ , giving  $333$  solutions.**Case 2**:    $y=0$ This gives  $4x + 2z = 2000$ . For every  $x$  from  $1$  to  $499$ , we get a  $z$  that works therefore we have  $499$  solutions.**Case 3**:    $z=0$ This gives  $4x + 3y = 2000$ . We have that  $4 \mid y$ , therefore we have solutions from  $y=4$  to  $y=664$  which are divisible by  $4$ , giving  $166$  sols. 

Now, we have to include the cases where  $x=y=0$  and  $y=z=0$ . Note we can't have  $x=z=0$  since  $3 \nmid 2000$ . In total, we have  $333 + 499 + 166 + 2 = 1000 \equiv \boxed{0} \pmod{1000}$ .

