Let  $2009=n=2m+1$ .

If  $a_1,\ldots,a_{2m+1}$  are the numbers on the circle and for  $1\le i\le n$ ,  $x_i\ge0$  denotes the number of times we increase the numbers at  $i$  and  $i+1$  by  $1$ , then taking indices  $\pmod{2m+1}$ , the numbers are equal in the end iff  $a_i+x_{i-1}+x_i=a_{i+1}+x_i+x_{i+1}$  for all  $i$ , i.e.
\begin{align*}
x_2-x_{2m+1} &= a_1-a_2 
x_4-x_2 &= a_3-a_4 
&\cdots 
x_{2m-2}-x_{2m-4} &= a_{2m-3}-a_{2m-2} 
x_{2m}-x_{2m-2} &= a_{2m-1}-a_{2m}
\end{align*}and
\begin{align*}
x_{2m-1}-x_{2m+1} &= a_{2m+1}-a_{2m} 
x_{2m-3}-x_{2m-1} &= a_{2m-1}-a_{2m-2} 
&\cdots 
x_3-x_5 &= a_5-a_4 
x_1-x_3 &= a_3-a_2 
\end{align*}(The "missing" condition,  $x_{2m}-x_1 = a_1-a_{2m+1}$  is redundant.) We want to find the smallest  $k_m$  such that for any  $(a_i)_{i=1}^{2m+1}\in\{0,1,\ldots,100\}^{2m+1}$ , there exists a solution  $(x_1,\ldots,x_{2m+1})$  to the system such that  $x_1+\cdots+x_{2m+1}\le k_m$ .

First we show that  $k_m\ge 100m(m+1)$ . Indeed, for  $(a_1,\ldots,a_{2m+1})=(100,0,100,0,\ldots,100,0,100)$ , we must have  $x_{2j}=x_{2m+1-j}=x_{2m+1}+100j$  for all  $1\le j\le m$ , so here
\begin{align*}
x_1+\cdots+x_{2m+1}
&=(2m+1)x_{2m+1}+100(1+2+\cdots+m)+100(1+2+\cdots+m) 
&\ge 100m(m+1),
\end{align*}as desired.

It remains to show that  $k_m=100m(m+1)$  suffices. Note that for any  $(a_1,\ldots,a_{2m+1})\in\{0,1,\ldots,100\}^{2m+1}$ , there exists a solution with  $\min\{x_1,\ldots,x_{2m+1}\}=0$  (indeed, just take any integer solution and translate everything so that the smallest becomes zero and everything else nonnegative). Under proper rotation, suppose WLOG  $x_{2m+1}=0$ . Then it's easy to check that  $|a_i-a_{i+1}|\le 100$  for all  $i$  forces  $x_{2j},x_{2m+1-2j}\le 100j$  for  $1\le j\le m$ , so this minimal solution must have
\[x_1+\cdots+x_{2m+1}\le 0+100(1+2+\cdots+m)+100(1+2+\cdots+m)=100m(m+1),\]whence  $k_m=100m(m+1)$  does indeed suffice.

