    label(string(i), (i,-2.1), align=S);
}
[/asy]
There's also the automorphism \(f(a) = n + 1 -  a\)
In this case, if \(n\) is odd, then it sends \(\frac{n+1}{2}\) to itself,
or that if \(n\) is even, then \(f\left(\frac{n}{2}\right) = \frac{n}{2} + 1\) and \(f\left(\frac{n}{2} + 1\right) = \frac{n}{2}\). 
[asy]
size(200);
int n = 7;
draw((1,0) -- (n,0));
draw((1,-2) -- (n,-2));
for(int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
    draw((i,-0.1) -- (n+1-i,-1.9), blue, arrow=EndArrow);
}
for(int i = 1; i <= n; ++i){
    filldraw(circle((i,0),0.07),black,white);
    filldraw(circle((i,-2),0.07),black,white);
    label(string(i), (i,0.1), align=N);
    label(string(i), (i,-2.1), align=S);
}
[/asy]</details>
Then for every automorphism \(f: P_n \rightarrow P_n\) then either \(\exists a. \quad f(a) = a\) or \(\exists a, b. \quad f(a) = b \; \land \; f(b) = a\); the problem statement.

With this in mind, we analyze the general case.**Let** \(T\) be a finite tree.
Pick paths \(L_1, \dots, L_k \subseteq T\) such that they have the maximum length \(l\) out of all paths in \(T\).**Then** every two paths \(L_i\) and \(L_j\) intersect:
Assume they don't.
[asy]
size(200);
int n = 7;
draw((1,0) -- (n,0), blue);
draw((1,-1) -- (n,-1), green);
draw((round(n/2),0) -- (round(n/2),-1), purple);
label(" $\vdots$ ", (round(n/2), -1/4), W, purple);
for(int i = 1; i <= n; ++i){
	filldraw(circle((i,0),0.07),darkgreen,white);
    filldraw(circle((i,-1),0.07),darkblue,white);
}
label(" $L_i$ ", (1/4,0), W, darkgreen);
label(" $L_j$ ", (1/4,-1), W, darkblue);
draw(box((1/2,1/2),(round(n/2)+1/2,-3/2)),red);
draw(box((round(n/2)-1/2,1/2-1/8),(n+1/2,-3/2+1/8)),orange);
[/asy]
As \(T\) is connected then there must be a path from a vertex of \(L_i\) to a vertex of \(L_j\). (note: the purple line indicates a path, not just one edge. sorry)
The length of the path in the red box is \(\le l\),
and the length of the path in the orange box is \(\le l\).
Then the number of vertices in the picture in total is \(\le 2l - 2\), a contradiction as there are \(\ge 2l\) vertices already present.**Then** as \(T\) has no cycles and \(L_1 \dots L_k\) intersect with each other, and thus \(L_1 \dots L_k\) has a common intersection:
<details><summary>Details</summary>Assume that there are \(k \ge 2\) paths. For the \(k = 1\) path case we skip this.
\(L_1\) and \(L_2\) intersect.
Induct on \(3 \le i \le k\), that for all \(i\) paths with length \(l\), then they have a common intersection.
Let, for instance \(L_1, \dots, L_{i - 1}\) has a common intersection \(A\) (set of vertices).
If \(L_i\) has no common intersection with \(A\),
[asy]
scale(200);
draw((-2,0) -- (5,0), red);
draw(rotate(60) * (-2,0) -- rotate(60) * (5,0), green);
draw(rotate(-60) * (-2,0) + rotate(60) * (2,0) -- rotate(-60) * (5,0) + rotate(60) * (2,0), blue);
for(int i = -2; i <= 5; ++i){
	filldraw(circle((i,0),0.08),darkred,white);
    filldraw(circle(rotate(60) * (i,0),0.08),darkgreen,white);
    filldraw(circle(rotate(-60) * (i,0) + rotate(60) * (2,0),0.08),darkblue,white);
}
[/asy]
let \(L_2, \dots ,L_{i - 1}, L_i\) intersect at \(B\),
and \(L_1, L_3, \dots, L_{i - 1}, L_i\) intersect at \(C\).
Then \(A \cap B = \varnothing\) as if there is any, then \(L_i\) has a common intersection with \(A\).
Same with \(A \cap C = \varnothing\) and \(B \cap C = \varnothing\).
Then there are vertices \(a \in A, b \in B, c \in C\) that are distinct from each other, and that
\(a\) has a path to \(b\) through \(L_1\), and \(b\) has a path to \(c\) through \(L_i\), and \(c\) has a path through \(c\) through \(L_2\).
Then this forms a cycle, a contradiction.</details>

As this is also a tree (acyclic) thus \(L_1 \cup \dots \cup L_n\) (let this be \(A\)) must also form a path (otherwise there is a cycle).
Anyway the isomorphism \(f\) must send paths to the same length; as \(l\) is the maximum path so it sends \(L_i\) to any of \(L_1, \dots, L_k\).
So \(A\) must be sent to \(L_1 \cup \dots \cup L_n = A\),
so \(f(A) = A\), thus \(f\) limits to an isomorphism \(A \rightarrow A\), and as \(A\) is itself a path,
by the case of paths, there is either an \(a \in A\) such that \(f(a) = a\) or that there exists \(a, b \in A\) such that \(f(a) = b\) and \(f(b) = a\).
We are done. \(\Box\). Whew!</details>

