The solutions are  $(a,b) = (1,0),(2,1)$ , which easily work.

Let   $$  3^a + 7^b = x^2  $$  where  $x \in \mathbb Z_{>0}$ . Of course neither of  $a,b$  can be negative (otherwise  $x$  won't be integral). If  $b=0$ , then   $$  3^a = x^2 - 1 = (x+1)(x-1)  $$   
So  $x+1,x-1$  are positive and powers of  $3$  (including  $1$ ) and they differ by  $2$ . It directly follows  $x+1=3$  and  $x-1=1$ . Thus  $x=2$  and  $a=1$ . So  $\boxed{(a,b) = (1,0)}$  is a solution. 

Assume  $b \ge 1$  now. Note the QR mod  $7$  are  $1,2,4$ . This forces  $a$  be even. Write  $a=2c$ . Then,   $$  7^b = x^2 - 3^{2c} = (x+3^c)(x-3^c)  $$  Again,  $x+3^c,x-3^c$  must be positive and powers of  $7$  (including  $1$ ). Since there difference is not divisible by  $7$ , so at least one of them equals  $1$ . It follows  $x-3^c = 1$ . Then,   $$  7^b = x + 3^c = 2 \cdot 3^c + 1  $$  Thus  $3^c \mid 7^b - 1$ . Using LTE (Lifting the Exponent Lemma) we get   $$ 3^{c-1} \mid b \implies b \ge 3^{c-1} $$   Hence, 
\begin{align*}
2 \cdot 3^c + 1 = 7^b \ge 7^{3^{c-1}} \ge 7^c \implies \left( \frac{7}{3} \right)^c \le 2 + \frac{1}{3^c}
\end{align*}
So we must have  $c=1$ , which corresponds to the solution  $\boxed{(a,b) = (2,1)}$ . This completes the proof.  $\blacksquare$

