<span style="color:#f00">**<u>*Case iii.*</u>**</span>  $p \neq 5$  or  $2$  and  $q \neq 5$  or  $2$ Note that  $\gcd(5,q)= 1$  and  $\gcd(5,p) = 1$  as well. Hence we have that,  $$ 5^p + 5^q\equiv 5 + 5^q \equiv 1 + 5^{q-1} \equiv \pmod{p} $$  \begin{align*}
\implies 5^{q-1} & \equiv -1\pmod{p} 
5^{2q-2} & \equiv 1\pmod{p} 
\end{align*}
From **Fermat's Little theorem** we get that,  $$ 5^{p-1}\equiv 1\pmod{p} $$  Which implies that,
\begin{align*}
