Let  $X,Y$  denote the tangents to  $(AH)$  from  $B$ .

We claim that  $A,X,P$  and  $A,Y,Q$  are collinear. To see this, let  $\phi_A$  be the inversion at  $A$  with power  $AH\cdot AD$ . We know that  $(AFP)$  tangent to  $BC$ , so  $\phi_A((AFP))=B\phi_A(P)$  is tangent to  $\phi_A(BC)=(AH)$ . Thus,  $\phi_A(P)=X$ , so  $A,X,P$  collinear. Similarly  $A,Y,Q$  collinear.

In fact, we have much more. We see that  $(AFP)$  is tangent to  $BC$ , so  $BF\cdot BA=BP^2$ . But  $BF\cdot BA=BX^2$ , so  $BP=BX$ . Similarly,  $BY=BQ$ , so  $BP=BX=BY=BQ$ . Therefore,  $X$  and  $Y$  lie on  $(PQ)$ , so  $QX\perp AP$  and  $PY\perp AQ$ . But  $\angle HXP=\angle HDP=90$  since  $HXDP$  is cyclic (because  $\phi_A(H)=D$  and  $\phi_A(X)=P$ ), so in fact  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $APQ$ , and  $B$  is the midpoint of  $PQ$ . Finally, we see that  $(XY;HE)=-1$ , so projecting through  $A$  gives us  $(PQ;DC)=-1$ , meaning that  $C$  is the harmonic conjugate of the foot of the altitude of  $A$  in the reference frame of  $APQ$ . In particular,  $X,Y,C$  are collinear.

We claim that  $QF$  and  $PE$  concur at  $DX\cap(AH)$ . Let  $\phi_P$  be the inversion at  $P$  with power  $PH\cdot PY$ . Note that  $YHDQ$  is cyclic, so  $\phi_P(D)=Q$ . We have  $\phi_P(DX\cap(AH))=(QAP)\cap(AH)$ . But we see that  $\phi_A((QAP)\cap(AH))=YX\cap BC=C$ , so
\[\phi_P(DX\cap(AH))=(QAP)\cap(AH)=\phi_A(C)=E.\]
Thus,  $P,E,DX\cap(AH)$  are collinear. Similarly, if  $\phi_Q$  is the inversion at  $Q$  with power  $QH\cdot QX$ , we can derive that  $\phi_Q(DX\cap(AH))=F$ , so  $Q,F,DX\cap(AH)$  are collinear, as desired.  $\blacksquare$

