Note that since  $B$  lies on the radical axis of  $(AFP)$  and  $(AFQ)$ , we have  $BP^2=BF \times BA =BQ^2$ .**<span style="color:#f00">Lemma:</span>**  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle APQ$ .**<span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span>** Since  $H$  lies on the  $A$ -altitude of  $\triangle ABC$ , it is well-known that it suffices to prove that   $$ DP \times DQ = AD \times HD. $$   (The proof is to just reflect  $H$  over  $PQ$ , also LIOG Delta 1.3) Since  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC$ , we have  $AD \times HD = BD \times DC$ , so we need to show  $DQ \times DP = BD \times DC$ . 

However, note   $$ DQ \times DP = (BQ+BD) \times (BP-BD) = BP^2-BD^2=BF \times BA-BD^2=BD \times BC-BD^2=BD \times DC $$   as desired.  $\square$ Now we invert about a circle centered at  $B$  with radius  $\sqrt{BF \times BA} = BP$ . Note that  $P$  and  $Q$  map to themselves, and pairs  $(F,A),(H,E)$  map to each other. Thus, line  $PE$  maps to  $(BPH)$  and line  $QF$  maps to  $(BQA)$ . Then  $X=QF \cap PE$  maps to  $X'=(BPH) \cap (ABQ) \neq B$ . Also note that  $(AEHF)$  maps to itself, so it suffices to prove that  $X'$  lies on  $(AEHF)$ .

Now note that   $$ \measuredangle AX'H = \measuredangle AX'B + \measuredangle BX'H = \measuredangle AQB + \measuredangle BPH = \measuredangle AQP + \measuredangle QPH = 90^\circ $$   since  $AQ \perp PH$  by our lemma. Thus,  $X' \in (AEHF)$  so we're done.

**Claim**:  $QZ\parallel PE$ , where  $Z=AC\cap (AFQ)$ .
*Proof.* It is sufficient to prove  $\frac{CE}{CZ}=\frac{CP}{CQ}$ . Let  $G$  be the foot of  $A$  on  $BC$ . By power of point we have  $BQ^2=BP^2=BF\cdot BA=BG\cdot BC$ . Which means  $CP\cdot CQ=CG\cdot CB$  (from EGMO) which is also equal to  $CH\cdot CF$ , means  $PQHF$  is cyclic, where  $H$  is the orthocenter. So
    \begin{align*}
        CE\cdot CA&=CH\cdot CF=CP\cdot CQ\tag{1}
        CA\cdot CZ&=CQ^2\tag{2}
    \end{align*}
    Dividing  $(1)$  by  $(2)$  we get our desired result.
So  $\angle CEP=\angle CZQ=\angle (AB, QE)$ . Hence the intersection of  $PE$  and  $QF$  lies on  $(AEF)$ .

