I assume that the overlines mean vectors. But I am not using cyclicity of \(ABCD\).
Let \(\vec m = \vec{AB}\), \(\vec n = \vec{CD}\), \(M, N\) be the midpoints of \(AB, CD\), respectively and \(\vec p = \vec{MN}\). Then \(\vec{MP}=\alpha \vec m, \vec{NS}=\alpha \vec n, \vec{MQ}=\beta \vec m, \vec{NR}=\beta\vec n\) for some reals \(\alpha, \beta\). Then
\begin{align*}
\overline{PR} \cdot \overline{AB} + \overline{QS} \cdot \overline{CD}
&= (\beta\vec n-\alpha\vec m +\vec p)\cdot \vec m + (\alpha\vec n-\beta\vec m+\vec p)\cdot \vec n   
&= \alpha (\vec n\cdot \vec n - \vec m\cdot \vec m) + \vec p(\vec m+\vec n).
\end{align*}
The right summand is constant so we minimize the left summand \(\alpha (AB^2-CD^2)\). If \(AB>CD\) then \(\arg \min \alpha=0\) and we should choose \(P=M, Q=N\), \(S, T\) arbitrarily. For \(AB=CD\) choose \(P, Q, R, S\) arbitrarily. Else there is no minimum.

Let  $P(x) = x^4 - x^3 + 2x^2 + 1$ . Idea: find infinitely many primes  $p$  such that the equation  $P(x) \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$  has no solution. This, of course, solves the problem. This claim is actually a direct consequence of the following theorem:**Theorem.**

Let  $P \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$  be an irreducible polynomial of degree  $\ge 2$ . There are infinitely many primes  $p$  such that the equation  $P(x) \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$  has no solutions.

The theorem is a well-known consequence of the Chebotarev Density Theorem, as has been discussed several times in AOPS.

However, there is an elementary solution to proving the theorem for this special case of  $P(x) = x^4 - x^3 + 2x^2 + 1$ , as we shall see:

EDIT: Unfortunately, the solution below does not work.
<details><summary>Incorrect solution</summary>Let  $Q(x) = x^2 + 1$  and  $R(x) = 64x^6 - 64x^4 + 1$ . By Nagell, there exists infinitely many primes  $p$  such that the equations  $R(x) \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$  and  $Q(y) \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ . We shall prove that any sufficiently large  $p$  of this forms suits our needs, that is, the equation  $P(x) \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$  does not have solutions.

Let  $b$  be such that  $R(b) \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ . Also, let  $a = \frac{-1}{16b^3}$  and  $c = \frac{-1}{8b^2}$ , both which are well defined modulo  $p$  for  $p > 2$ , since  $b \not\equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ . It is easy to check that

\begin{align*}
P(x) \equiv (x^2 - \frac{x}{2} + c)^2 + (ax + b)^2 \pmod{p}
\end{align*}

Now, if  $P(x) \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ , we have  $y^2 + z^2 \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ . Since  $p$  is a prime divisor of  $Q$ , this is possible only if  $y \equiv z \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ . EDIT: this is the incorrect part - we have guaranteed  $p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$ , when we would actually want  $p \equiv 3 \pmod{4}$ .

 Therefore, if  $P$  has a root  $x$  modulo  $p$ , we have

\begin{align*}
x^2 - \frac{x}{2} + c \equiv ax + b \equiv 0 \pmod{p}
\end{align*}

It is now easy to calculate that this requires  $2^{11}b^{10} - 2^6b^6 - 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ . However, the greatest common divisor of the polynomials  $R$  and  $2^{11}x^{10}- 2^6x^6 - 1$  is a constant, so this can happen only for small enough  $p$ , those dividing the GCD. This completes the solution.</details>

