<details><summary>Official Solution, according to the Solutions PDF</summary>Using the geometric series formula, we can expand the integral as follows: \begin{align*}\int_0^{2\pi}\dfrac{1}{a+b\cos(\theta)}d\theta &=\dfrac{1}{a}\int_0^{2\pi} 1 + \dfrac{b}{a}\cos(\theta)+\left(\dfrac{b}{a}\right)^2\cos^2(\theta)d\theta)&= \dfrac{1}{a}\sum_{n=0}^\infty \int_0^{2\pi}\left(\dfrac{b}{a}\right)^n \left(\dfrac{e^{i\theta}+e^{-i\theta}}{2}\right)^n d\theta&=\dfrac{2\pi}{a}\sum_{n=0}^\infty \left(\dfrac{b^2}{a^2}\right)^n\dfrac{\binom{2n}{n}}{2^{2n}}d\theta\end{align*}

To evaluate this sum, recall that  $C_n=\dfrac{1}{n+1}\dbinom{2n}{n}$  is the  $n$ th Catalan number.  The generating function for the Catalan numbers is \[\sum_{n=0}^\infty C_n x^n=\dfrac{1-\sqrt{1-4x}}{2x},\] and taking the derivative of  $x$  times this generating function yields  $\displaystyle\sum\dbinom{2n}{n}x^n=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-4x}}$ .  Thus the integral evaluates to  $\dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt{a^2-b^2}}$ , as desired.</details>

