**Solution:**Let  $i$  be some element in  $\{1,2,\cdots,k-1\}.$  We are given that  $n\mid a_i(a_{i+1}-1),$  which is equivalent to having  $a_i(a_{i+1}-1)\equiv0\pmod{n}$ , so that   $$ a_i\equiv a_ia_{i+1}\pmod{n}. $$   As a result, for any  $i,j\in\{1,2,\cdots,k-1\}$  such that  $i<j$ , we obtain that   $$ x_ix_{i+1}\cdots x_j\equiv x_ix_{i+1}\cdots(x_jx_{j+1})\pmod{n}. $$   
Repeating this  $k-j$  times gives   $$ x_ix_{i+1}\cdots x_j\equiv x_ix_{i+1}\cdots x_k\pmod{n}, $$   so setting  $i=j$  implies that   $$ x_i\equiv x_ix_{i+1}\cdots x_k\pmod{n}. $$  Now suppose by contradiction that  $n$  divides  $a_k(a_1-1)=a_1a_k-a_k$ , so that  $a_k\equiv a_1a_k\pmod{n}.$  But then, using our above results, we see that
\begin{align*}
a_1&\equiv a_1a_2\cdots a_k\pmod{n}
&\equiv a_2a_3\cdots a_{k-1}(a_ka_1)\pmod{n}
&\equiv a_2a_3\cdots a_{k-1}a_k\pmod{n}
&\equiv a_2\pmod{n},
\end{align*}
contradicting the assumption that the  $a_i$  are distinct integers in the set  $\{1,2,\cdots,n\}.$  Thus our original claim that  $n\mid a_k(a_1-1)$  is incorrect and the result follows.  $\blacksquare$

Assume by contradiction  $a_k a_1 - a_k \equiv 0~(\text{mod } n)$ . We begin with the following:**<span style="color:blue">Claim:</span>** For  $i = 2,~3,~\ldots~, k$ , we have  $n \mid a_1 a_i - a_1$ .

We prove this using induction. Our base case,  $i = 2$ , is given.

We wish to prove  $i = j+1$ , or  $n \mid a_1 a_{j+1} - a_1$ , given  $i = j$  holds:

\begin{align*}
0 &\equiv (a_{j+1} - 1)(a_1 a_j - a_1) 
&\equiv (a_1 (a_j a_{j+1}) - (a_1 a_j)) - (a_1 a_{j+1} - a_1) 
&\equiv (a_1 a_j - a_1) - (a_1 a_{j+1} - a_1) 
&\equiv -(a_1 a_{j+1} - a_1) \quad (\text{mod } n).~{\color{blue} \blacksquare}
\end{align*}

Thus we know  $j = k$  holds, and by our initial assumption \[a_1 \equiv a_k a_1 \equiv a_k~(\text{mod } n),\] which is a contradiction, as  $a_1$  and  $a_k$  cannot differ by a multiple of  $n$ .  $\blacksquare$

