Notice that because  $M, K$  are the midpoints of  $QP, PB$ , respectively, we have  $MK\parallel QB\implies \angle{AQM}=\angle{QMK}$ . But because  $PQ$  is tangent to circle  $\Gamma$ , we have  $\angle{QMK}=\angle{MLK}$ , so  $\angle{AQP}=\angle{AQM}=\angle{MLK}$ . Similarly, we have  $\angle{APQ}=\angle{APM}=\angle{PML}=\angle{MKL}$ , so  $\triangle{MLK}\sim\triangle{AQP}$  by AA similarity.

Let  $\omega_1$  be the circumcircle of  $\triangle{ABC}$  and  $R$  be the circumradius. Because  $M, K$  are midpoints of  $QP, PB$ , we have  $QB=2MK$  and similarly because  $M, L$  are midpoints of  $PQ, QC$ , we have  $PC=2ML$ . From  $\triangle{MLK}\sim \triangle{AQP}$  we have  $AQ\cdot MK=AP\cdot ML$ , so by power of a point we have \begin{eqnarray*}R^2-OQ^2=\text{Pow}_{\omega_1}(Q)=AQ\cdot QB=AQ\cdot 2\cdot MK=AP\cdot 2\cdot ML=AP\cdot PC=\text{Pow}_{\omega_1}(P)=R^2-OP^2,\end{eqnarray*}and  $OQ=OP$  as desired.</details>

