**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $\triangle APQ \sim \triangle MKL$ .
*Proof.* Observe that  $KM$  and  $ML$  are midlines of  $\triangle PQB$  and  $\triangle PQC$ , thus  $KM \parallel AB$  and  $ML \parallel AC$ . Consequently,   $$ \measuredangle MLK = \measuredangle QMK = \measuredangle PMA = \measuredangle PQA $$  and a similar process gives  $\measuredangle MKL = \measuredangle APQ$ .  $\square$ Note that again because of midlines,  $ML = 0.5 \cdot PC$  and  $MK = 0.5 \cdot QB$ . By similar triangle ratios, 
\begin{align*}
\frac{AP}{MK} &= \frac{AQ}{ML}
AP \cdot ML &= AQ   \cdot MK
AP \cdot \left(0.5 \cdot PC\right) &= AQ \cdot (0.5 \cdot QB)
AP \cdot PC &= AQ \cdot QB
\end{align*}
which implies  $\text{Pow}_{(ABC)} (P) = \text{Pow}_{(ABC)} Q$ , so  $OP=OQ.$   $\blacksquare$

In what follows,  $\Gamma$  is the circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$ *Claim:*  $\triangle APQ \sim \triangle MKL$ *Proof:* As  $K$  and  $M$  are the midpoints of the lines  $PB$  and  $PQ$  respectively, mid point theorem gives us  $MK \parallel AB$  and  $2MK = QB$ . Similarly, applying midpoint theorem again gives us  $ML \parallel PC \implies 2ML = PC$ . Since  $PQ$  is tangent to  $\Gamma$  at the point  $M$  we get  $\angle{MKL}=\angle{LMP}=\angle{APQ}$  and  $\angle{MLK}=\angle{KMQ}=\angle{AQP}$ . Hence we get that  $\triangle APQ \sim \triangle MKL$ [rule]
Since we have proved that the triangles are similar, we can now safely say the following 
\begin{align*} 
\frac{AP}{MK} &= \frac{AQ}{ML}  
\implies AP \cdot ML &= AQ \cdot MK  
\implies AP \cdot 2ML &= AP \cdot 2MK  
\implies AP \cdot PC &= AQ \cdot QB
\end{align*}  
From the above manipulations, it follows that the powers of the point  $P$  and  $Q$  with respect to the point  $O$  are equal.  Therefore if the radius of  $\Gamma$  is  $s$  then \begin{align*} 
OP^2 &- s^2  
OQ^2 &- s^2  
\implies OP &= OQ
\end{align*}
which is the required result.

