We let  $\omega$  be the circumcircle of ( $ABC$ ).

Our solution is based off the following lemma.

<span style="color:#f00"> $\Delta AQP \sim \Delta MLK$ </span>

    Since,  $PQ$  is tangent to  $\Gamma$  so,  $\angle KLM = \angle KMQ$  and  $\angle LKM = \angle LMP$ .

    We know that
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{PM}{PQ} = \frac{PK}{PB} = \frac{1}{2}
        \frac{QM}{QP} = \frac{QL}{QC} = \frac{1}{2}
    \end{align*}
    So,  $MK \parallel QB$  and  $ML \parallel PC$  (By Similarity).
    From this we get, 
    \begin{align*}
    &\angle AQP = \angle AQM = \angle KMQ = \angle KLM 
    &\angle APQ = \angle APM = \angle LMP = \angle LKM
    \end{align*}

    So,  $\Delta AQP \sim \Delta MLK$ .

This gives us
\begin{align}
    \frac{AQ}{ML} = \frac{AP}{MK}
\end{align}
Since  $\Delta QML \sim \Delta QPC$  and  $\Delta PMK \sim \Delta PQB$  (Proved in the lemma), we get
\begin{align}
    \frac{ML}{PC} = \frac{MK}{BQ} = \frac{1}{2}
\end{align}

From multiplying <span style="color:#00f">Eq 1</span> and <span style="color:#9a00ff">Eq 2</span>
\begin{align*}
    &\Rightarrow \frac{AQ}{PC} = \frac{AP}{BQ} 
    &\Rightarrow AQ \cdot BQ = AP \cdot PC
\end{align*}

So, 
\begin{align*}
&\Rightarrow \text{Pow}_{\omega}(Q) = AQ \cdot BQ = AP \cdot PC = \text{Pow}_{\omega}(P) 
&\Rightarrow R^2 - OQ^2 = \text{Pow}_{\omega}(Q) = \text{Pow}_{\omega}(P) = R^2 - OP^2
\end{align*}

Hence,  $OQ = OP$ .

Complex bash:

Toss onto the complex plane. Let  $(ABC)$  be the unit circle. Since  $p$  and  $q$  lie on chords, we know that \[p+ac\overline{p}=a+c \quad \text{ and } \quad q+ab\overline{q}=a+b.\] We now make the following claim:

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span>Triangles  $APQ$  and  $MKL$  are similar.
*Proof.*Note that \[\triangle CQP \sim \triangle LQM\] and \[\triangle BPQ \sim \triangle KPM\] for obvious reasons (note that they are both in the ratio  $2:1$ ). In addition, we can say that \[\angle APQ = 180-\angle CPQ = 180-\angle LMQ = \angle K\] and similarly, \[\angle AQP = 180-\angle BQP = 180-\angle KMP = \angle L.\] Thus, we know that  $\triangle APQ \sim \triangle MKL$ , as required.  $\square$ Using this claim, we know that:
\begin{align*}
\overline{\dfrac{p-a}{q-a}} &= \dfrac{k-m}{l-m} 
&= \dfrac{\tfrac{p+b}{2}-\tfrac{p+q}{2}}{\tfrac{c+q}{2}-\tfrac{p+q}{2}} 
&= \dfrac{\tfrac{b-q}{2}}{\tfrac{c-p}{2}} 
&= \dfrac{b-q}{c-p} 
\dfrac{\overline{p}-\tfrac{1}{a}}{\overline{q}-\tfrac{1}{a}} &= \dfrac{b-q}{c-p} 
(c-p)\left(\overline{p}-\tfrac{1}{a}\right) &= (b-q)\left(\overline{q}-\tfrac{1}{a}\right) 
c\overline{p}-\tfrac{c}{a}-\left|p\right|^2+\tfrac{p}{a} &= b\overline{q}-\tfrac{b}{a}-\left|q\right|^2+\tfrac{q}{a} 
\tfrac{a+c-p}{a}-\tfrac{c}{a}-\left|p\right|^2+\tfrac{p}{a} &= \tfrac{a+b-q}{a}-\tfrac{b}{a}-\left|q\right|^2+\tfrac{q}{a} 
\left|p\right|^2  &= \left|q\right|^2
\end{align*}
so we are done.  $\square$

