abc(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{(2a + b + c)^2} + \frac{1}{(a + 2b + c)^2} + \frac{1}{(a + b + 2c)^2}\right) \leq \frac{3(ab + bc + ca)}{16}.
\end{align*}

Now, dehomogenize by assuming that  $a + b + c = 3$ . The inequality collapses to \begin{align*}
abc\left(\frac{1}{(a + 3)^2} + \frac{1}{(b + 3)^2} + \frac{1}{(c + 3)^2}\right) \leq \frac{ab + bc + ca}{16},
\end{align*}

or equivalently \begin{align*}
\frac{1}{(a + 3)^2} + \frac{1}{(b + 3)^2} + \frac{1}{(c + 3)^2} \leq \frac{1}{16a} + \frac{1}{16b} + \frac{1}{16c}.
\end{align*}

We will show that \begin{align*}
\frac{1}{16a} + \frac{1}{16b} + \frac{1}{16c} - \frac{1}{(a + 3)^2} - \frac{1}{(b + 3)^2} - \frac{1}{(c + 3)^2} \geq 0.
\end{align*}

Define  $f(x) = \frac{1}{16x} - \frac{1}{(x + 3)^2}$ . We can easily compute \begin{align*}
f'(x) = -\frac{1}{16x^2} + \frac{2}{(x + 3)^3}
\end{align*}

and \begin{align*}
f''(x) = \frac{1}{8x^3} - \frac{6}{(x+3)^4}.
\end{align*}

We see that  $f''(x)$  is clearly greater than or equal to  $0$  over the interval  $[0,3]$ , because  $(x + 3)^4 \geq 48x^3$  over this interval. Hence,  $f$  is convex. 

By Jensen's, we therefore obtain that  $f(a) + f(b) + f(c) \geq 3f(1) = 0$ , as desired.

