 $(a)$ \begin{proof}
We proceed by induction on  $n$ , the number cards, to show that a game with such moves necessarily end with no matter what initial state. 
Base Case:  $n=1$ , this is obvious. 
Assume that for  $n-1$  cards, the game necessarily end. Then denote the leftmost card of  $n$  cards  $K$ . Notice that if  $K$  is already black, we are done since by the inductive hypothesis, the game necessarily finish on the remaining  $n-1$  cards, thus assume that  $K$  is initially gold. Then if we are to perform a move on  $K$  at any point, it changes to black. Then for the rest  $n-1$  cards, it again ends. If we are never to perform a move on  $K$ , the game also necessarily ends for the rest  $n-1$  cards, since there is only one move that may change the state of  $K$ . Therefore we complete our induction and we are done. 
\end{proof} $(b)$ \begin{proof}
We claim that player two wins no matter what move the two players take. Consider labelling the cards  $1, 2\cdots, 2009$  from right to left. Then by condition, the game ends only when all the cards with labels  $50i$  where  $i$  is a nonneg integer are all black. Then notice that every move can only turn one and only one card with label multiple of  $50$  over. Also observe that there are  $40$  cards with label multiple of  $50$  on the table. Therefore, in order to turn all of then over, we need an even number moves, implying that player two always make the last move no matter what. 
\end{proof}

