(a) Let us label the cards with the weights  $2^{2008}, 2^{2007}, \ldots, 2, 1$  from left to right. Then, since
\begin{align*}
2^k &> 2^{k-1}+\ldots+2+1 
&> 2^{k-1}+\ldots+2^{k-49}
\end{align*}
for all  $49 \le k \le 2008,$  we know that the total weight of the cards turned from gold to black must be greater than the total weight of the cards turned from black to gold in every operation.

Hence, the total sum of the weights of the gold cards is a strictly decreasing monovariant, but it is bounded below by  $0.$  Therefore, the game must end in a finite number of turns.  $\square$ (b) I claim that the answer is no, i.e. there does not exist any winning strategy for the player that goes first. It suffices to show that no matter what the two players do, the second player is forced to win.

Let us number the cards  $1$  to  $2009$  from left to right. Now, consider the cards numbered  $10, 60, \ldots, 1960,$  of which there are  $40.$  Note that all of these cards must be turned black in order for the game to end. Also, it is not difficult to see that every move flips exactly one of these cards. Therefore, since there are an even number of these cards, it follows that there will be an odd number of them showing their gold side after each of the first player's moves.

