Let  $BI$  meet  $EF$  are  $L$ , and let  $CI$  meet  $EF$  at  $K$ . We will use the following lemma.

<span style="color:#f00">**Lemma:**</span> We have  $\angle BKC = \angle BLC = 90^{\circ}$ .

*Proof:*  We will prove one of the angle identities as the other follows by symmetry. Note that \[ \measuredangle KDB = \measuredangle KDC = \measuredangle CEK = \measuredangle AEK = \measuredangle KFA = \measuredangle KFB .\] Thus,  $KIDB$  is cyclic, which implies  $\angle BKI = \angle BKC = 90^{\circ}$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Thus, we have that  $Q = BK \cap CL$  is the orthocenter of  $BIC$ . Note that  $Q$  is also the  $D$ -excenter of  $\triangle DKL$ . Also,  $B$  and  $C$  are the  $K$  and  $L$  excenters, which implies  $I$  is the incenter of  $DKL$  as it is the intersection of  $DH$ ,  $BL$ , and  $CK$ . To finish, we will use the following well-known fact.

<span style="color:#f00">**Lemma:**</span> In a triangle  $ABC$  with  $A$ -excenter  $I_A$  and intouch triangle  $DEF$ , let  $S$  be the midpoint of the  $A$ -altitude. Then,  $S$ ,  $D$ , and  $I_A$  are collinear.

*Proof:*  Let  $K$  be the foot of the altitude from  $A$  to  $BC$ . Let  $X$  the extouch point on  $BC$ . By homothety at  $A$ ,  $AD$  meets the excircle a second time at point  $Y$  which is on  $XI_A$ . Then,  $\triangle AKD \sim \triangle YXD$ , and they are related by a homothety at  $D$ . Thus, a homothety at  $D$  sends  $S$  to  $I_A$ , so  $S$ ,  $D$ , and  $I_A$  are collinear.   $\blacksquare$ The foot of the perpendicular from  $I$  to  $EF$  is precisely the midpoint of  $EF$ . Using the lemma,  $PH$  bisects  $EF$ , as desired.

Let \(Q=\overline{BI}\cap\overline{EF}\cap(BC)\) and \(R=\overline{CI}\cap\overline{EF}\cap(BC)\) by the Iran lemma. Since \(DQR\) is the orthic triangle of \(\triangle BIC\), \(I\) is its incenter and \(H\) is its \(D\)-excenter. If \(N\) denotes the midpoint of \(\overline{EF}\), \(N\) is tangency point of the incircle of \(\triangle DQR\) and \(\overline{QR}\), so the collinearity is by the well-known ``midpoints of altitudes'' lemma.

