label(" $\alpha$ ", S, dir(170)*5);
label(" $\beta$ ", S, dir(135)*7.5);
dot(" $A$ ", A, SW);
dot(" $A'$ ", B, dir(B));
dot(" $J$ ", J, W);
dot(" $S$ ", S, dir(S));
[/asy] One can easily show that  $\angle JA'S = \theta - \beta$ . Apply Sine rule in  $\triangle JAS$  and  $\triangle JSA'$ . This would yield 
\[\frac{\overline{JA}}{\sin \alpha} = \frac{\overline{SJ}}{\sin(\theta + \alpha)} \qquad \text{and} \qquad \frac{\overline{JA'}}{\sin \beta} = \frac{\overline{SJ}}{\sin(\theta - \beta)}.\] 
Since the length of the train is the same, we also have that  $\overline{JA} = \overline{JA'}$ . One can finally divide out the equations eliminating all the lengths to get 
\[\sin \beta \sin(\theta + \alpha) = \sin \alpha \sin(\theta - \beta).\] 
From here on, its a basic trigonometric manipulations with sum to product and other basic formulae. From the above equation, observe that \begin{align*} \cos(\theta + \alpha - \beta) - \cos(\alpha + \beta + \theta)           & = \cos(\alpha + \beta - \theta) - \cos(\alpha + \theta - \beta)  \iff 2\cos(\theta + \alpha - \beta)                                     & = \cos(\alpha + \beta - \theta) + \cos(\alpha + \beta + \theta)  \iff \cos(\theta + \alpha - \beta)                                      & = \cos(\alpha + \beta)\cos \theta                                \iff \cos \theta\cos(\alpha - \beta) - \sin \theta \sin(\alpha - \beta) & = \cos \theta \cos(\alpha + \beta)                               \iff \cos(\alpha - \beta) - \tan \theta \sin(\alpha - \beta)            & = \cos(\alpha + \beta)                                           \iff \tan \theta                                                        & = \frac{2\sin \alpha \sin \beta}{\sin(\alpha - \beta)} \end{align*} where the final thing can be derived easily by expanding the  $\cos$  terms out. This finishes the proof.  $\blacksquare$

