Let  $ A \equal{} \{a_1,a_2,...,a_n\}$  where  $ a_1 < a_2 < \cdots < a_n$ , we have  $ a_1 \plus{} a_2 < a_1 \plus{} a_3 < \cdots < a_1 \plus{} a_n$  are  $ n \minus{} 1$  pairwise distinct elements of  $ A \plus{} A$ , thus  $ |A \plus{} A| \geq n \minus{} 1$ . On the other hand,  $ |A \minus{} A| \leq {n \choose 2} \equal{} \frac {n^2 \minus{} n}{2}$ , the equality holds when we choose every  $ a_{i \plus{} 1}$  large enough compared to  $ a_i$  for  $ i \equal{} 1,2,\cdots,n \minus{} 1$ . Thus:  $ |A \minus{} A| \minus{} |A \plus{} A| \leq \frac {n^2 \minus{} n}{2} \minus{} (n \minus{} 1)$ . Hence, it is enough to find  $ c$  such that  $ \frac {n^2 \minus{} n}{2} \minus{} (n \minus{} 1) < n^2 \minus{} cn^{\frac {8}{5}}$ . But this is equivalent to  $ c < \frac {\frac {n^2}{2} \plus{} \frac {3n}{2} \minus{} 1}{n^{\frac {8}{5}}}$ . Now the existence of  $ c$  is followed because  $ \lim_{n\to \plus{} \infty}\frac {\frac {n^2}{2} \plus{} \frac {3n}{2} \minus{} 1}{n^{\frac {8}{5}}} \equal{} \plus{} \infty$ . QED.

