The answer is  $k=67$ .

Let  $f(k)=\binom{100}{k}\binom{200}{k}$ . Then for  $k=1,2,\ldots,99$ , \begin{align*}
f(k+1)-f(k) &= \binom{100}{k+1}\binom{200}{k+1}-\binom{100}{k}\binom{200}{k}

&= \frac{100-k}{k+1}\binom{100}{k}\cdot\frac{200-k}{k+1}\binom{200}{k}-\binom{100}{k}\binom{200}{k}

&= \binom{100}{k}\binom{200}{k}\left(\frac{100-k}{k+1}\cdot\frac{200-k}{k+1}-1\right)

&= \binom{100}{k}\binom{200}{k}\left(\frac{19999-302k}{(k+1)^2}\right).
\end{align*} 
Note  $19999-302k>0$  for  $k=1,2,\ldots,66$  and  $19999-302k<0$  for  $k=67,68,\ldots,100$ . Thus  $f(k)$  increases, attains a maximum at  $k=67$ , and then decreases thereafter.

Shu solved the problem assuming the incorrect wording, which a lot of people assumed it was.  It was meant to imply the 4th largest from the bottom (if that makes sense) so it should be  $10\left\lfloor4\times\frac{90-5}{6}+4\right\rfloor=\boxed{606}$ .

A different argument is that there are  $\binom{k-1}{3}$  ways to choose the three numbers less than  $k$  and  $90-k$  ways to choose the number greater than  $k$ .  Thus, the expected value is  $\frac{1}{\binom{90}{5}\sum_{i=4}^{89}k(90-k)\binom{k-1}{3}}$ .  Finding the summation, we have
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=4}^{89}k(90-k)\binom{k-1}{3}&=\sum(90-k)(4)\binom{k}{4}
&=4\sum (91-(k+1))\binom{k}{4}
&=4\sum91\binom{k}{4}-4\sum5\binom{k+1}{5} 
&=4\cdot91\sum\binom{k}{4}-4\cdot5\sum\binom{k+1}{5}
\end{align*}
By the Hockey Stick Identity, we have  $364\binom{90}{5}-20\binom{91}{6}=\left(364-20\times\frac{91}{6}\right)=\left(\frac{182}{3}\right)\binom{90}{5}$ .  Thus, the expected value is  $\frac{182}{3}\approx60.6$  so the answer is  $\boxed{606}$ .

